<section class="page-header">
  <h1>History</h1>
  <p class="muted">How the Urdaneta City Public Library continues to serve the community.</p>
</section>

<section class="timeline card">
  <div class="timeline-intro">
    <p>
      Established in 1938 under Mayor Patricio Abenojar, the Urdaneta City Public Library--originally known as
      the "Citizens Foundation Library"--holds the distinction of being recognized by Speaker Jose Yulo as the
      first municipal library in the Philippines. Although the original facility was completely destroyed during
      World War II, it was successfully revived and professionalized through the leadership of figures like
      Mayor Manuel N. Sison and long-time librarian Trinidad V. Abenojar-Mudo. By its 40th anniversary in 1978,
      the library had evolved from a small dream of Atty. Wilfredo G. Ambrosio into a thriving independent
      institution, housing over 3,000 volumes and overseeing several barangay branches to serve the growing
      educational needs of the Urdaneta community.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Timeline History:</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="timeline-list">
    <div class="timeline-item reveal">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-content">
        <h3>The Early Years &amp; Establishment</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1937:</strong> Atty. Wilfredo G. Ambrosio, then Municipal President, envisions the library, but the dream is deferred due to a lack of funds.</li>
          <li><strong>1938:</strong> Under the first term of Mayor Patricio Abenojar, the library is officially established via Resolution No. 193. It is named the "Citizens Foundation Library."</li>
          <li><strong>1938-1941:</strong> The library is recognized by Speaker Jose Yulo as the first Municipal Library in the Philippines.</li>
          <li><strong>WWII Era:</strong> The library is completely destroyed during the war but is later reopened by Mayor Abenojar following the liberation of the islands.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item reveal">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-content">
        <h3>Post-War Development &amp; Growth</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1948:</strong> Mayor Manuel N. Sison streamlines the library's operations.</li>
          <li><strong>1948-1956:</strong> The library is managed by Atty. Wilfredo E. Ambrosio, Jr., who was appointed by the Secretary of Education.</li>
          <li><strong>June 16, 1956:</strong> Mrs. Trinidad V. Abenojar-Mudo (a Library Science graduate) is appointed as the Municipal Librarian.</li>
          <li><strong>1973:</strong> Due to increasing volumes of books and readers, the position of Municipal Assistant Librarian is created. Mrs. Carmen Brolis-Bokier is appointed to the role.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item reveal">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-content">
        <h3>Expansion &amp; The 1978 Status</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1978:</strong> The library celebrates 40 years of existence.</li>
          <li><strong>Facility Status:</strong> By this time, the library has moved out of the Municipal Building into a separate building financed by the NRC, PACD, and local funds.</li>
          <li><strong>Inventory:</strong> The collection grows to 3,092 volumes of books, with an average of 102 daily readers.</li>
          <li><strong>Reach:</strong> The Municipal Library becomes responsible for overseeing six barangay (school) libraries across Urdaneta's 34 barangays.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

